#########################################################################
# ==============================================================================
# PICK THE REGION BY HAND
# ==============================================================================
# select_roi() pops up a 3D view of a measured surface and lets the operator
# draw the area that is actually going to be scanned.
#
# WHY. The YOLO detector's box can be several times the size of the part: an
# 83 x 165 mm plate has come back as a 135 x 287 mm box enclosing the optical
# breadboard, the clamps and pins holding the plate down, and a stepped mount
# block. Everything inside the box is then measured by the Keyence, meshed and
# rastered -- a 198 k-triangle mesh only 30% of whose vertices are the plate, a
# 412 k-pose eddy-current toolpath where about 45 000 poses over the plate alone
# would have done, and tool poses 89.8 degrees off vertical where Noether runs
# over the rim of the mount block.
#
# A detector that is wrong by that much is not fixable by tightening a
# threshold, and it does not have to be: there is an operator standing there,
# the surface is on screen, and drawing a box round a plate takes two seconds.
#
# WHERE IT GOES IN THE PIPELINE. After stage 2 of run_automated_scan(), the
# Azure rough mesh -- the first 3D surface that exists, and only 40 s of the
# run. The region it returns then constrains stage 3 (the 10-minute Keyence
# raster) and stage 5 (the toolpath). Doing it after stage 3 would mean the
# expensive measurement has already been paid for.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SELECTION IS DRAWN ON ONE SENSOR'S MESH AND APPLIED TO ANOTHER'S
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which is the whole difficulty. The operator draws on the AZURE rough mesh at
# stage 2; what gets cropped is the KEYENCE mesh at stage 5. So of the four
# things this returns, only some of them travel:
#
#   mask       DOES NOT TRAVEL. One entry per AZURE vertex, and the Keyence mesh
#              of the same part has a different vertex count and ordering
#              entirely. Exact where it fits and unusable elsewhere.
#   bounds3d   ITS z DOES NOT TRAVEL. The Azure reads this cell 11.6-13.6 mm too
#              far, enough that the ROI's z floor lands ABOVE the top of the
#              Keyence mesh of the same part -- no overlap at all, and a 3D-box
#              crop then keeps zero faces. It is PROVENANCE only, reported and
#              never applied.
#   region     travels. A rectangle in world x-y, on whole selection cells and
#              clipped to the taught work area -- NOT the raw span of the
#              selected vertices, which is quantised by the mesh's 6 mm sampling
#              and has come back with zero extent on one axis for a band the
#              operator drew several eddy-current lines wide. See
#              roi_select_geom.selection_region().
#   footprint  travels, and is the one to use: the DRAWN SHAPE rasterised onto a
#              2 mm grid in world x-y, PLUS the height of the selected surface in
#              each of those cells. It keeps what a rectangle throws away -- the
#              corner of an L-shaped part is not in it, and a rectangle round an
#              L-shaped part contains bench -- and its z is per cell rather than
#              one global range, so a 1-3 mm sensor-to-sensor disagreement in
#              surface height cannot empty a crop.
#
# noether_path.dpi's _gate_roi_shape() reads them in that order of preference,
# and crop_mesh_to_roi() is what actually down-selects a mesh with one.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE FOOTPRINT CARRIES HEIGHTS AND NOT JUST A SHADOW
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Because z cannot be dropped from the decision altogether. A region selected on
# top of a flat plate must not let the Keyence scan part of the table under it,
# and the parts here will not stay flat: a curved specimen has to stay inside its
# ROI in z as well, to within a sensible tolerance.
#
# So each footprint cell holds the z of the surface the operator selected there,
# and a crop keeps a face when its cell is in the footprint AND its height is
# within GATE_ROI_Z_TOLERANCE_M (6 mm, derived from the measured sensor
# disagreement) of that cell's height. On a flat plate that band is a slab; on a
# curved part it follows the curve. What it is never is one global z range for
# the whole selection: a global range can miss the other sensor's surface
# completely and keep no faces at all.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE WINDOW IS A SEPARATE PROCESS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not a Qt widget in this interpreter. Four reasons, any one of which is
# sufficient:
#
#   * ECAutomatedScan.dgp does include(dgpy, "Qt.dpi", prefer_pyqt=False) and
#     runs spatialnde2's OpenSceneGraph viewer on the Qt main thread. A second
#     binding in one process is a segfault looking for a reason.
#   * The stage that calls this runs in a WORKER THREAD. A bare thread has no
#     dgpy context, module print() from one never reaches the console, and any
#     module-derived object crossing into it drags OpaqueWrappers that make
#     censoring block forever. A Qt window opened from there hits all of those
#     at once.
#   * It must BLOCK until the operator decides. Blocking the Qt main thread is
#     how the viewer stops repainting; blocking anything else while pumping a
#     second event loop is worse.
#   * VTK on a software GL stack over VNC does occasionally take a process
#     down. A scan that has already flown an Azure sweep must not be one of
#     them.
#
# So: a temp directory, a request JSON in, a result JSON plus a .npy mask and a
# .ply sub-mesh out, and subprocess.run() with a timeout. Exit 0 accepted, 10
# cancelled, anything else is a failure with the child's output attached. Same
# trade noether_path.dpi's mesh_uv_blender.py already makes for Blender.
#
# The selection logic lives in roi_select_geom.py (numpy only, no Qt, no VTK)
# and the window in roi_select_ui.py. That split is what lets roi_select_test.py
# cover every tool -- rectangle, lasso, brush, and the deselect variant of each
# -- on a headless box, testing the same functions the operator's mouse calls
# rather than a second implementation of them.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE OPERATOR SEES
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The surface shaded by height (blue low, red high), unselected dimmed,
# selected in GREEN -- green because the height ramp has no green in it at any
# height, and a red highlight would be indistinguishable from the tallest thing
# in the scene.
#
#   1 Navigate   left drag orbits, middle pans, right/wheel zooms
#   2 Rectangle  drag a box
#   3 Lasso      drag freehand, or click corners and Enter/right-click to close
#   4 Brush      drag to paint; [ and ] resize, Shift+wheel also
#
#   Ctrl+drag    DESELECT with whichever tool is active
#   Shift+drag   add instead of replace
#   Ctrl+A / Ctrl+D / Ctrl+I / Ctrl+Z   all / clear / invert / undo
#   T F S I R    top, front, side, isometric, refit
#   Enter        Accept        Esc   Cancel
#
# Middle and right drag always move the camera whatever tool is active, so
# looking at something never costs a mode switch. It opens in TOP view, which
# is the one a footprint is chosen in.
#
# "near surface only" (on by default) keeps, in each patch of screen, only what
# is nearest the camera -- so a box drawn over the part does not also take its
# underside or the bench straight behind it. It is plain numpy, not a z-buffer
# read or vtkHardwareSelector, because those are the first things to misbehave
# on the software GL stack the cell is driven through over VNC.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEPENDENCIES
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# python3-pyqt5 and python3-vtk9 in the interpreter that runs the CHILD, which
# is sys.executable unless ROI_SELECT_PYTHON says otherwise. Both are packaged
# on Ubuntu 22.04 and RViz usually pulls them in already. roi_select_available()
# probes for them and says what is missing rather than failing at the moment the
# operator is waiting.
#
# NOTE it is `import vtkmodules...`, never `import vtk`: the top-level shim
# imports every module VTK was built with, and on an Arch host that dies on a
# missing libopenvr_api.so despite VTK being perfectly usable.

import json
import os
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

import numpy as np


# ==============================================================================
# CONFIGURATION
# ==============================================================================

#: Master switch. False makes select_roi() refuse and
#: refine_region_interactively() pass the region through untouched, which is
#: the headless / unattended path.
#:
#: Seeded from ROI_SELECT if the config set one BEFORE including this file, so
#: the switch can live up at the top of ECAutomatedScan.dgp with the other
#: technician constants where somebody will actually find it, instead of two
#: thirds of the way down next to an include.
global ROI_SELECT_ENABLED
ROI_SELECT_ENABLED = bool(globals().get("ROI_SELECT", True))

#: How long the operator gets before the child is killed. Generous on purpose:
#: this blocks a scan that has already flown a sweep, and an operator who walks
#: away mid-selection should find the window still there. The timeout exists so
#: a wedged child cannot hold the cell forever, not to hurry anybody.
global ROI_SELECT_TIMEOUT
ROI_SELECT_TIMEOUT = 3600.0

#: Interpreter for the child. None means sys.executable. Set this if dgpy is
#: running under a python that has no PyQt5 while another one does.
global ROI_SELECT_PYTHON
ROI_SELECT_PYTHON = None

#: Keep the temp directory after a successful selection. The sub-mesh is
#: written there, so anything that wants to hold on to it should copy it out --
#: which is what save_roi() does.
global ROI_SELECT_KEEP
ROI_SELECT_KEEP = False

#: Brush radius in SCREEN PIXELS, remembered between calls in a session.
global ROI_SELECT_BRUSH_PX
ROI_SELECT_BRUSH_PX = 40.0

#: The last accepted selection, so a stage that failed after one can be retried
#: without asking the operator to draw it again.
global last_roi
last_roi = None


# ==============================================================================

global roi_select_module_dir
def roi_select_module_dir():
    """Where the helper scripts are. Same directory as this .dpi."""
    here = globals().get("SCAN_MODULE_DIR")
    if here and os.path.exists(os.path.join(here, "roi_select_ui.py")):
        return here
    # The .dpi has no __file__ of its own, so fall back to a module that does.
    # noether_path.dpi resolves its own directory the same way.
    for probe in (globals().get("_noether_module_file", lambda: None)(),
                  __file__ if "__file__" in globals() else None):
        if probe:
            d = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(probe))
            if os.path.exists(os.path.join(d, "roi_select_ui.py")):
                return d
    raise IOError("cannot find roi_select_ui.py -- set SCAN_MODULE_DIR")


#: Cached probe results, keyed by (interpreter, DISPLAY). A dict rather than a
#: rebound name because a .dpi body is a function body and its top-level names
#: are closure variables to the defs inside it -- mutating is fine, rebinding
#: from in there would need a nonlocal.
_roi_probe = {}


global roi_select_available
def roi_select_available(python=None, recheck=False):
    """(True, why) if the window can actually be opened right now.

    IT ACTUALLY BUILDS A QApplication in the child rather than looking at
    environment variables, and the difference is not academic. A session with
    WAYLAND_DISPLAY set and DISPLAY unset passes a "DISPLAY or WAYLAND_DISPLAY"
    heuristic, and the child then dies on SIGABRT with no output at all, because
    the PyQt5 packaged here has only the xcb platform plugin. A probe that
    answers "yes" and is wrong is worse than no probe: the caller uses this to
    decide whether an unattended run may carry on, and a false yes turns a
    working headless scan into a crash.

    Checked BEFORE a scan commits to anything, so "PyQt5 is not installed on
    this machine" is read at the start of a run rather than 40 seconds in with
    the arm parked over the part. The answer is cached per interpreter and
    DISPLAY, so asking twice in one scan costs one launch.
    """
    if not ROI_SELECT_ENABLED:
        return False, "ROI selection is switched off (ROI_SELECT_ENABLED)"
    try:
        d = roi_select_module_dir()
    except IOError as e:
        return False, str(e)

    exe = python or ROI_SELECT_PYTHON or sys.executable
    key = (exe, os.environ.get("DISPLAY"), os.environ.get("WAYLAND_DISPLAY"))
    if not recheck and key in _roi_probe:
        return _roi_probe[key]

    probe = ("import sys, PyQt5.QtWidgets as W, "
             "vtkmodules.vtkRenderingOpenGL2, "
             "vtkmodules.qt.QVTKRenderWindowInteractor; "
             "W.QApplication(sys.argv[:1])")
    try:
        out = subprocess.run([exe, "-c", probe], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                             stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, timeout=120,
                             env=_child_env())
    except Exception as e:
        res = (False, "cannot run %s (%s)" % (exe, e))
        _roi_probe[key] = res
        return res

    if out.returncode == 0:
        res = (True, "ready (%s, %s)" % (exe, d))
    else:
        text = out.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip().splitlines()
        if text:
            why = text[-1]
        else:
            # Qt aborts SILENTLY when it cannot load a platform plugin, which
            # on a headless or Wayland-only session is the usual reason, so
            # there is nothing to quote and the guess has to be spelled out.
            why = ("it aborted with no output (signal %d), which normally "
                   "means no usable X display: DISPLAY=%r. Over VNC set "
                   "DISPLAY to the VNC server's screen."
                   % (-out.returncode, os.environ.get("DISPLAY")))
        res = (False, "%s cannot open a window: %s. Needs python3-pyqt5 and "
                      "python3-vtk9." % (exe, why))
    _roi_probe[key] = res
    return res


def _child_env():
    """The child's environment, with the PARENT'S Qt stripped out of it.

    dgpy has already loaded PySide6 in this process, and PySide6 exports
    QT_PLUGIN_PATH and friends pointing at ITS OWN plugin tree. Inherited by a
    PyQt5 child those produce "could not load the Qt platform plugin xcb",
    which reads like a missing package and is not one.
    """
    env = dict(os.environ)
    for var in ("QT_PLUGIN_PATH", "QT_QPA_PLATFORM_PLUGIN_PATH",
                "QML2_IMPORT_PATH", "QML_IMPORT_PATH", "QT_QPA_PLATFORM",
                "QT_API", "PYQTGRAPH_QT_LIB"):
        env.pop(var, None)
    # The child imports roi_select_geom from its own directory; nothing else of
    # the parent's PYTHONPATH should be able to shadow it.
    env["PYTHONNOUSERSITE"] = "1"
    return env


def _taught_work_area():
    """The taught work volume's x-y as (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax), or None.

    THE CLIP THAT KEEPS ROUNDING FROM GROWING MOTION. select_roi() reports a
    region rounded outward to whole selection cells, which is how a thin drawn
    band stops collapsing to zero extent -- and outward is toward more motion, so
    the result is held inside what was actually taught.

    None when the cell declares no box (work_cell.dpi's documented state on
    hardware that has not been touched off yet): no clip is better than a clip to
    made-up numbers, and mesh_reconstruction.dpi checks the region against the
    volume again before anything moves.
    """
    volume = globals().get("work_volume")
    if not callable(volume):
        return None
    try:
        got = volume(quiet=True)
    except Exception as e:                                       # noqa: BLE001
        print("  (no work area to clip the region to: %s: %s)"
              % (type(e).__name__, e))
        return None
    if got is None:
        return None
    _frame, lo, hi = got
    return (float(lo[0]), float(lo[1]), float(hi[0]), float(hi[1]))


def _write_input(source, workdir):
    """A PLY on disk for the child to read, and a note of what it was.

    A path is used where it lies -- no copy of a 68 MB reconstruction. Arrays
    are written out, which is what lets this be called on something held in
    memory rather than on a file.
    """
    import roi_select_geom as geom

    if isinstance(source, str):
        if not os.path.exists(source):
            raise IOError("no such mesh or point cloud: %s" % (source,))
        return source, source

    verts = tris = None
    if isinstance(source, (tuple, list)) and len(source) == 2:
        verts, tris = source
    elif isinstance(source, np.ndarray):
        verts = source
    elif hasattr(source, "vertices") and hasattr(source, "triangles"):
        verts, tris = np.asarray(source.vertices), np.asarray(source.triangles)
    else:
        raise TypeError(
            "select_roi() takes a PLY path, an (N, 3) vertex array, or a "
            "(vertices, triangles) pair -- not %s" % (type(source).__name__,))

    verts = np.asarray(verts, dtype=np.float64)
    if verts.ndim != 2 or verts.shape[1] != 3:
        raise ValueError("vertices must be N x 3, got %s" % (verts.shape,))
    path = os.path.join(workdir, "roi_input.ply")
    geom.write_ply(path, verts, None if tris is None else np.asarray(tris))
    return path, "<%d points in memory>" % (len(verts),)


global select_roi
def select_roi(mesh_or_points, title=None, initial=None, timeout=None,
               front_only=True, brush_radius=None, python=None, keep=None,
               work_area=None, line_spacing=None):
    """Ask the operator to draw the region to scan. Blocks. None if cancelled.

    mesh_or_points   a PLY path (mesh OR point cloud), an (N, 3) vertex array,
                     or a (vertices, triangles) pair.
    initial          (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax) in world metres, preselected --
                     hand it the detector's region so the operator CORRECTS a
                     selection instead of drawing one from nothing.
    work_area        (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax) the region is CLIPPED to. None
                     takes the cell's taught work volume; False turns the clip
                     off, which is for tests on made-up coordinates.
    line_spacing     what the region has to be wide enough to scan at, metres.
                     None takes SCAN_LINE_SPACING. A selection too narrow to
                     hold two passes is refused in the window, where the operator
                     can still redraw it.

    Returns a dict, every part of which some downstream stage wants:

      region     (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax) world metres. THE TUPLE THE PIPELINE
                 ALREADY CONSUMES -- reconstruct_fine(region=),
                 plan_ec_path(region=) -- in their order, which is NOT
                 (xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax). Transposing the two has sent a sweep
                 696 mm away from the part. Whole selection cells, clipped to
                 the work area: see roi_select_geom.selection_region() for why it
                 is not the raw span of the selected vertices, which on a 6 mm
                 mesh has come back with zero extent on one axis.
      mask       bool ndarray over the INPUT vertices, in input order. Exact,
                 and usable only on the surface it was drawn on.
      footprint  THE DRAWN SHAPE IN WORLD x-y, on a 2 mm grid, AND THE HEIGHT OF
                 THE SELECTED SURFACE IN EACH CELL -- the part of a selection
                 that can be applied to a DIFFERENT mesh, which is what the
                 pipeline needs (drawn on the Azure, applied to the Keyence).
                 The heights are what put z back into the crop as a band that
                 follows the surface rather than a global box. Packed;
                 roi_select_geom.footprint_decode() and noether_path.dpi's
                 crop_mesh_to_roi() both read it.
      submesh    a PLY of just the selected surface, re-indexed. This is the
                 one that lets us stop rastering the bench: a region is still a
                 rectangle, and a rectangle around an L-shaped part still
                 contains the bench in the corner.
      bounds3d   (xmin, ymin, zmin, xmax, ymax, zmax). PROVENANCE ONLY -- where
                 the surface the operator was looking at sat in z, on the sensor
                 that measured it. Never a crop; see the note at the top. z DOES
                 reach the crop, but per cell through `footprint`, not as this
                 global range.
      plus n_selected / n_total / submesh_triangles / footprint_cells / source.

    Cancelling returns None and that means STOP, not "carry on with what you
    had" -- the caller decides what to do about it. To run with no operator at
    all, do not call this: ROI_SELECT_ENABLED = False, or pass
    roi_select=False to run_automated_scan().
    """
    import roi_select_geom as geom

    ok, why = roi_select_available(python=python)
    if not ok:
        raise RuntimeError("cannot open the region selector: %s" % (why,))

    keep = ROI_SELECT_KEEP if keep is None else bool(keep)
    timeout = ROI_SELECT_TIMEOUT if timeout is None else float(timeout)
    if brush_radius is None:
        brush_radius = ROI_SELECT_BRUSH_PX

    if initial is not None:
        initial = [float(v) for v in initial]
        if len(initial) != 4:
            raise ValueError("initial must be (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax), got %s"
                             % (initial,))

    if work_area is None:
        work_area = _taught_work_area()
    if line_spacing is None:
        line_spacing = globals().get("SCAN_LINE_SPACING")

    workdir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="roi_select_")
    try:
        src, described = _write_input(mesh_or_points, workdir)
        req = {"input": src,
               "title": title or "Select the region to scan",
               "initial": initial,
               "output": os.path.join(workdir, "result.json"),
               "mask": os.path.join(workdir, "mask.npy"),
               "submesh": os.path.join(workdir, "roi_submesh.ply"),
               "brush_radius": float(brush_radius),
               "front_only": bool(front_only),
               # The two limits the window needs and cannot work out for itself.
               # False rather than None is how a caller says "no clip"; the child
               # reads None as "no clip" too, so False lands as null in the JSON.
               "work_area": (None if not work_area
                             else [float(v) for v in work_area]),
               "line_spacing": (None if line_spacing is None
                                else float(line_spacing))}
        reqfile = os.path.join(workdir, "request.json")
        with open(reqfile, "w") as fh:
            json.dump(req, fh, indent=1)

        exe = python or ROI_SELECT_PYTHON or sys.executable
        helper = os.path.join(roi_select_module_dir(), "roi_select_ui.py")
        print("  waiting for the operator to pick a region (%s)" % (described,))
        proc = subprocess.run([exe, helper, reqfile], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                              stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, timeout=timeout,
                              env=_child_env())
        out = proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace")

        if proc.returncode == 10:
            print("  the operator cancelled the region selection")
            return None
        if proc.returncode != 0 or not os.path.exists(req["output"]):
            raise RuntimeError("the region selector failed (exit %d):\n%s"
                               % (proc.returncode, out[-4000:]))

        with open(req["output"]) as fh:
            res = json.load(fh)
        res["region"] = tuple(float(v) for v in res["region"])
        if res.get("region_drawn") is not None:
            res["region_drawn"] = tuple(float(v) for v in res["region_drawn"])
        res["bounds3d"] = tuple(float(v) for v in res["bounds3d"])
        res["mask"] = np.load(req["mask"])

        if keep:
            res["workdir"] = workdir
        else:
            # The sub-mesh is the only thing worth surviving the temp dir, and
            # a caller that never looks at it should not leak a directory per
            # scan. Move it beside the input, which is a scan directory.
            dest = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(src)),
                                "roi_submesh.ply")
            try:
                shutil.move(res["submesh"], dest)
                res["submesh"] = dest
            except Exception as e:
                print("  WARNING: could not keep the ROI sub-mesh (%s); it is "
                      "at %s until this process exits" % (e, res["submesh"]))
                res["workdir"] = workdir
                keep = True

        globals()["last_roi"] = res
        print("  region: %s" % (describe_roi(res),))
        return res
    finally:
        if not keep:
            shutil.rmtree(workdir, ignore_errors=True)


global describe_roi
def describe_roi(roi):
    """One line for the console and the scan log."""
    if roi is None:
        return "no region selected"
    x0, y0, x1, y1 = roi["region"]
    line = ("%.1f x %.1f mm at x %.3f..%.3f y %.3f..%.3f -- %d of %d points "
            "(%.1f%%)" % ((x1 - x0)*1e3, (y1 - y0)*1e3, x0, x1, y0, y1,
                          roi["n_selected"], roi["n_total"],
                          100.0*roi["n_selected"]/max(1, roi["n_total"])))
    # The rectangle above is whole selection cells; the points it was measured
    # from span less. Worth a log line, because their difference is the entire
    # reason a thin drawn band no longer comes back with zero extent.
    rd = roi.get("region_drawn")
    if rd is not None and (abs(rd[0] - x0) > 1e-12 or abs(rd[1] - y0) > 1e-12
                           or abs(rd[2] - x1) > 1e-12
                           or abs(rd[3] - y1) > 1e-12):
        line += ("; the selected points themselves span %.1f x %.1f mm, rounded "
                 "out to whole cells and clipped to the work area"
                 % ((rd[2] - rd[0])*1e3, (rd[3] - rd[1])*1e3))
    if roi.get("submesh_triangles"):
        line += ", sub-mesh %d of %d triangles" % (
            roi["submesh_triangles"], roi.get("triangles_total", 0))
    # The footprint is the part of this that will be applied to ANOTHER mesh, so
    # it is the part worth seeing in the log. Its absence matters too: without
    # one the crop falls back to the rectangle above.
    if roi.get("footprint_described"):
        line += "; drawn shape %s" % (roi["footprint_described"],)
    elif roi.get("footprint") is None:
        line += ("; NO drawn footprint (%s) -- a crop will use the rectangle"
                 % (roi.get("footprint_error", "not recorded"),))
    fpe = roi.get("footprint")
    if fpe is not None and fpe.get("zrows") is None:
        line += ("; NO per-cell height map, so a crop will apply x-y alone")
    b = roi.get("bounds3d")
    if b:
        line += ("; measured at z %.4f..%.4f on this sensor -- that GLOBAL range "
                 "is provenance, not a crop; the crop's z is the footprint's "
                 "per-cell band" % (min(b[2], b[5]), max(b[2], b[5])))
    return line


global refine_region_interactively
def refine_region_interactively(region, surface, enabled=None, title=None,
                                on_cancel="raise", **kwargs):
    """The pipeline's entry point: correct `region` against `surface`, or don't.

    Returns (region, roi) where roi is None if the operator was never asked.
    The region always comes back usable, so the caller can pass it straight on
    to the next stage whatever happened here.

    CARRY THE `roi` ON, NOT JUST THE REGION. The region is a rectangle; the roi
    carries the drawn SHAPE, and it is what crop_mesh_to_roi() down-selects the
    measured mesh with at planning time -- plan_ec_path(roi=...) ->
    plan_mesh_path -> require_plannable_mesh. Drop it and the planner is back to
    rastering whatever the sensor saw inside a rectangle.

    enabled=None consults ROI_SELECT_ENABLED. If the selector cannot run at all
    -- no DISPLAY, no PyQt5 -- this SAYS SO AND CARRIES ON with the region it
    was given rather than stopping an unattended run. An operator cancelling is
    different: that is a deliberate "no", and on_cancel="raise" stops the scan.
    """
    enabled = ROI_SELECT_ENABLED if enabled is None else bool(enabled)
    if not enabled:
        print("  region selection skipped (ROI_SELECT_ENABLED is off); using "
              "the region as given")
        return region, None

    ok, why = roi_select_available()
    if not ok:
        print("  region selection unavailable, using the region as given: %s"
              % (why,))
        return region, None

    # initial=None: THE WINDOW OPENS WITH NOTHING SELECTED. Preselecting the
    # detected region makes Accept live the instant the window appears, so a
    # single stray keypress commits the whole part and starts the raster over it.
    # roi_select_ui.py also closes that keyboard hole, and an empty Accept asks
    # whether it means cancel, so the failure mode is a question rather than a
    # 300 mm raster.
    #
    # `region` is still passed to select_roi() as the region to fall back to when
    # the operator cancels (the return above), and is still what the pipeline
    # uses if ROI selection is off entirely.
    roi = select_roi(surface, title=title, initial=None, **kwargs)
    if roi is None:
        if on_cancel == "raise":
            raise RuntimeError(
                "the operator cancelled the region selection, so the scan is "
                "stopped. Re-run with roi_select=False (or set "
                "ROI_SELECT_ENABLED = False) to scan the detected region "
                "without being asked.")
        print("  region selection cancelled; using the region as given")
        return region, None
    return roi["region"], roi


global save_roi
def save_roi(roi, directory, name="roi"):
    """Copy a selection's sub-mesh and mask into a scan directory, and say where.

    The region tuple is in the scan's own log; the mask and the sub-mesh are
    what let a later session reproduce the selection instead of re-drawing it.
    """
    if roi is None:
        return None
    if not os.path.isdir(directory):
        os.makedirs(directory)
    out = {}
    mesh = os.path.join(directory, "%s_submesh.ply" % (name,))
    if roi.get("submesh") and os.path.exists(roi["submesh"]):
        if os.path.abspath(roi["submesh"]) != os.path.abspath(mesh):
            shutil.copyfile(roi["submesh"], mesh)
        out["submesh"] = mesh
    mask = os.path.join(directory, "%s_mask.npy" % (name,))
    np.save(mask, roi["mask"])
    out["mask"] = mask
    meta = os.path.join(directory, "%s.json" % (name,))
    with open(meta, "w") as fh:
        json.dump({k: (list(v) if isinstance(v, tuple) else v)
                   for k, v in roi.items() if k != "mask"}, fh, indent=1)
    out["json"] = meta
    return out
